 3m1s\nuser-mention-service-d8cbd6fcf-tbqbt      1/1     Running   0          3m1s\nuser-mongodb-6c75c9d7db-cvnvj             1/1     Running   0          3m1s\nuser-service-5786579998-dk9dh             1/1     Running   0          82s\nuser-timeline-mongodb-6ccb4447d8-r5gng    1/1     Running   0          3m1s\nuser-timeline-redis-546bbd644f-pmfrq      1/1     Running   0          3m1s\nuser-timeline-service-6479b8f4fd-s276t    1/1     Running   0          3m1s\nwrk2-job-ssrtm                            1/1     Running   0          83s\n', id='c2467d2d-d7d9-4ed8-962d-a9ef9e0b3bf5', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-83232fddde02674f'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='967b8f48-a801-41bc-83a1-70c164f706c8'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose a social network application composed of many microservices. So far, they\'ve used get_services, get_metrics, and exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to check the services, metrics, and pods. The get_metrics call for user-service showed no up instances, but the kubectl get pods showed user-service-5786579998-dk9dh is running. That\'s a contradiction. Maybe the metrics query was incorrect.\n\nNext step: Check why the user-service isn\'t showing up in metrics. Maybe the Prometheus query was wrong. The user used "up{job=\\"user-service\\"}" which might not be the correct job name. Need to verify the correct job name for user-service. Alternatively, check the service\'s metrics endpoint. Also, check if the service is actually exposed and reachable. Maybe use get_services again to confirm the service names. Wait, they already did get_services earlier. Or maybe check the deployment and service YAMLs for user-service to see if they\'re configured correctly. But the user can\'t edit files, so maybe use kubectl describe deployment and service for user-service. Also, check if there are any events or errors in the pods. The kubectl get pods shows the pod is running, but maybe there are logs indicating issues. So next steps: check the deployment details, service details, and logs for user-service. But the available tools are limited. The user can use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to describe the deployment and service, and get logs. Also, check if the service is properly exposed. So, next tool should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl describe deployment user-service -n social-network" and "kubectl describe service user-service -n social-network". Also, check logs with "kubectl logs user-service-5786579998-dk9dh -n social-network". But the user can only run one tool call per step. So first, describe the deployment to see if there are any issues with replicas or rollout status. Then, describe the service to check if it\'s correctly configured. Alternatively, check the logs for any errors. But the user might need to proceed step by step. Let\'s start with describing the deployment.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo investigate the user-service\'s status further, I need to examine its deployment configuration and events. The `kubectl describe deployment` command will show details about the deployment, including replica sets, pod statuses, and any errors during rollout. This will help identify if there are issues with the deployment itself, such as failed rollouts or misconfigurations.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 546, 'prompt_tokens': 2791, 'total_tokens': 3337, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe22-71e8-7c73-9d1d-017678c60412-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2791, 'output_tokens': 546, 'total_tokens': 3337}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='30ff77a4-ef4e-4de3-96fc-72ba7951e306'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in their social network application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they\'ve used get_services, get_metrics, and kubectl get pods. The pods are all running, but maybe there\'s a problem with the user-service since the initial metrics query for up{job="user-service"} returned no results. That suggests the user-service might not be healthy.\n\nThe next step is to check the deployment details for the user-service. Using kubectl describe deployment on the user-service in the social-network namespace would provide more information. The deployment might have issues like failed rollouts, missing replicas, or configuration errors. By examining the deployment\'s events and replica set status, they can determine if there\'s a problem with the user-service\'s deployment that\'s causing it to not be reachable or functioning correctly. This will help identify the root cause of the issue.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe deployment user-service -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9da520229e3fb3b1', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 221, 'prompt_tokens': 3772, 'total_tokens': 3993, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfe22-8bd7-7a22-b420-bd9825d574ca-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe deployment user-service -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9da520229e3fb3b