7017/TCP   4m32s\nuser-timeline-redis      ClusterIP      10.108.193.118   <none>                                     6379/TCP    4m32s\nuser-timeline-service    ClusterIP      10.101.86.176    <none>                                     9090/TCP    4m32s\n', id='204fd004-591d-4e70-a450-6ad575a087f7', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-94792beabad864b4'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0a33b330-454d-4331-8db8-0f0d8a801537'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service is listed in the services, so the Service resource exists. But the problem is that there are no metrics, traces, or dependencies. Wait, maybe the Service is correctly set up, but the application isn\'t actually handling the traffic. Or perhaps the pods aren\'t accessible through the Service.\n\nWait, the user mentioned that the pod is in a Running state, but there\'s no traffic. Maybe the Service is misconfigured. Let me check the Service details. The user-service has a ClusterIP, which is internal. But if other services are trying to reach it via the Service name, maybe the DNS resolution isn\'t working. Or maybe the ports aren\'t correctly exposed.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the Deployment for user-service isn\'t scaling properly, leading to no pods being available. Wait, the earlier check with get_pods showed the pod is running. But maybe there\'s a problem with the Service\'s selector. Let me check the Service\'s YAML to see if the labels match the pods. But since I can\'t directly view the YAML, maybe I can use the get_ops tool to see if there are any operations related to the user-service. Wait, the get_operations tool can list operations for a service. If there are no operations, that might indicate no traffic. But the user already tried get_traces and get_metrics, which returned nothing. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the Service is not properly exposed. For example, if the Service\'s port isn\'t correctly mapped to the container\'s port. Let me check the Service\'s configuration. Since I can\'t do that directly, maybe I should check the endpoints. Using kubectl get endpoints for the user-service to see if there are any endpoints associated. If there are no endpoints, that means the Service isn\'t routing traffic to any pods. That would happen if the pods don\'t match the Service\'s selector. \n\nSo the next step is to check the endpoints for the user-service. If there are no endpoints, the root cause is that the Service\'s selector isn\'t matching any pods, possibly due to label mismatches. Alternatively, maybe the Deployment isn\'t creating pods correctly. But the pod was reported as running. Hmm. Wait, maybe the pod is running but not ready. Let me check the pod\'s status again. But the user already checked get_pods and saw the pod was in Running state. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the application inside the pod isn\'t listening on the correct port. For example, if the container is listening on a different port than what\'s specified in the Service. But without checking the pod\'s configuration, it\'s hard to tell. \n\nSince the user has already checked the Services and found user-service exists, the next logical step is to check the endpoints to see if traffic is being routed. If there are no endpoints, that\'s a problem. So I should use the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to get the endpoints for the user-service.\n</think>\n\nThe `kubectl get endpoints` command will verify if the `user-service` has any endpoints (i.e., pods) associated with it. If no endpoints are listed, it indicates that the Service\'s selector is not matching any pods, or the pods are not properly exposed. This would explain the absence of metrics, traces, and dependencies, as no traffic is reaching the service.\n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_kubectl_cmd_safely", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get endpoints -n social-network user-service"}}\n</