e Service\'s YAML. The Service\'s name is user-service, namespace is social-network, selector is service=user-service, ports are 9090. The pods have the same labels. So why no endpoints?\n\nThis is perplexing. The only thing left is to check the actual Service YAML to see if there\'s a typo or misconfiguration. But since I can\'t do that directly, maybe I should check the Service\'s status again. The Service\'s endpoints are <none>, which indicates that no pods match the selector. But the Deployment\'s pods are running. This suggests a mismatch between the Service\'s selector and the pod\'s labels.\n\nWait, maybe the Service\'s selector is "service=user-service", but the pod\'s labels are "app=user-service" and "service=user-service". So the Service\'s selector is matching on "service=user-service", which is present. So that should work. Unless there\'s a typo in the Service\'s selector, like a space or a different key.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the Service is using a different label key. For example, if the Service\'s selector was "app=user-service" instead of "service=user-service", then it would match. But in this case, the Service\'s selector is "service=user-service", which is present. So that should work.\n\nI think the only possible explanation is that the Service\'s selector is not matching the pod\'s labels. But according to the information provided, it should. This is a contradiction. Maybe there\'s an error in the Service\'s configuration that\'s not visible here. Alternatively, maybe the pods are not actually running, but the get pods command shows they are. This is confusing.\n\nGiven that the Service\'s endpoints are <none>, the root cause is that the Service\'s selector is not matching any pods. Even though the Deployment\'s pods are running, the Service\'s selector is not matching their labels. Therefore, the Service is not routing traffic to the pods, leading to zero metrics, traces, and dependencies. The solution is to adjust the Service\'s selector 